                ],
                "description": {
                  "markdown": "Replace pickle-based bundle parsing in `portal/services.py:35` with a data-only\nformat such as JSON and validate that the decoded structure is a list of\nexpected row objects before creating `Ticket` records. If legacy pickle bundles\nmust remain supported, apply an authenticity check before deserialization and\nrestrict accepted types; the current code trusts raw request bytes as pickle\ninput.",
                  "text": "Replace pickle-based bundle parsing in `portal/services.py:35` with a data-only format such as JSON and validate that the decoded structure is a list of expected row objects before creating `Ticket`…"
                }
              }
            ],
            "function": "portal/services.py:import_handoff",
            "impact": "If an attacker can reach this route with arbitrary upload content,\n`pickle.loads()` may reconstruct attacker-chosen Python objects during request\nprocessing. Possible outcomes include server-side code execution in the\napplication process, unexpected object creation, and request failure on malformed\npayloads.",
            "prerequisites": "- The route `/imports/` must be reachable by an attacker or other untrusted\n  requester\n- Repo-visible evidence shows `portal/urls.py` exposes `views.imports`, and no\n  `login_required` or similar access-control wrapper was found\n- This is regular application code, not an optional extension or loadable plugin\n- The trust boundary is web request upload data crossing into server-side Python\n  object deserialization\n- The attacker model is a remote user able to submit a multipart file field\n  named `bundle`\n- The provided threat model describes the project as network-facing and\n  processing untrusted input, and it specifically prioritizes import and\n  deserialization flows creating `Ticket` rows\n- The related CVE reference is similar at the class level\n  (`CWE-502` / unsafe deserialization in Python applications), but the supplied\n  evidence does not indicate this code is a direct variant of a specific known\n  CVE",
            "recommendation": {
              "codeAfter": "31:     return Attachment.objects.create(ticket=ticket, original_name=upload.name, stored_name=name)\n32:\n33:\n34: def import_handoff(upload):\n35:     rows = __import__(\"json\").loads(upload.read().decode(\"utf-8\"))\n36:     if not isinstance(rows, list) or not all(isinstance(row, dict) for row in rows):\n37:         raise ValueError(\"bundle must be a JSON array of objects\")\n38:     created = []\n39:     for row in rows:\n40:         subject = row.get(\"subject\", \"Delivery follow-up\")\n41:         description = row.get(\"description\", \"\")\n42:         ticket = Ticket.objects.create(\n43:             reference=row.get(\"reference\") or slugify(subject)[:18],\n44:             customer_key=row.get(\"customer_key\", \"walkup\"),",
              "codeBefore": "31:     return Attachment.objects.create(ticket=ticket, original_name=upload.name, stored_name=name)\n32:\n33:\n34: def import_handoff(upload):\n35:     rows = pickle.loads(upload.read())\n36:     created = []\n37:     for row in rows:\n38:         subject = row.get(\"subject\", \"Delivery follow-up\")\n39:         description = row.get(\"description\", \"\")\n40:         ticket = Ticket.objects.create(\n41:             reference=row.get(\"reference\") or slugify(subject)[:18],\n42:             customer_key=row.get(\"customer_key\", \"walkup\"),",
              "fixDiff": "--- a/portal/services.py\n+++ b/portal/services.py\n@@ -31,12 +31,14 @@\n     return Attachment.objects.create(ticket=ticket, original_name=upload.name, stored_name=name)\n \n \n-def import_handoff(upload):\n-    rows = pickle.loads(upload.read())\n-    created = []\n-    for row in rows:\n-        subject = row.get(\"subject\", \"Delivery follow-up\")\n-        description = row.get(\"description\", \"\")\n+def import_handoff(upload):\n+    rows = __import__(\"json\").loads(upload.read().decode(\"utf-8\"))\n+    if not isinstance(rows, list) or not all(isinstance(row, dict) for row in rows):\n+        raise ValueError(\"bundle must be a JSON array of objects\")\n+    created = []\n+    for row in rows:\n+        subject = row.get(\"subject\", \"Delivery follow-up\")\n+        description = row.get(\"description\", \"\")\n         ticket = Ticket.objects.create(\n             reference=row.get(\"reference\") or slugify(subject)[:18],\n             customer_key=row.get(\"customer_key\", \"walkup\"),",
              "remediationBackwardCompat": "### Description\nIn portal/services.py, function import_handoff at line 35, replace pickle-based deserialization with JSON parsing and validate that the decoded payload is a list of dict-like records before iterating over it.\n\n### Suggested fix\n    return Attachment.objects.create(ticket=ticket, original_name=upload.name, stored_name=name)\n\n\ndef import_handoff(upload):\n    rows = __import__(\"json\").loads(upload.read().decode(\"utf-8\"))\n    if not isinstance(rows, list) or not all(isinstance(row, dict) for row in rows):\n        raise ValueError(\"bundle must be a JSON array of objects\")\n    created = []\n    for row in rows:\n        subject = row.get(\"subject\", \"Delivery follow-up\")\n        description = row.get(\"description\", \"\")\n        ticket = Ticket.objects.create(\n            reference=row.get(\"reference\") or slugify(subject)[:18],\n            customer_key=row.get(\"customer_key\", \"walkup\"),\n\n### Rationale\nJSON decoding materializes only basic data types and does not execute attacker-controlled constructors during parsing. Requiring a top-level list of objects preserves the function's expected data shape while preventing pickle from reconstructing arbitrary Python objects.\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, POST /imports/ with a multipart file field bundle containing a crafted pickle payload causes pickle.loads(upload.read()) to deserialize attacker-controlled bytes during import. After the change, the same pickle payload should fail JSON decoding or schema validation without deserializing Python objects, while a valid UTF-8 JSON array such as [{\"subject\":\"Test\",\"description\":\"ok\"}] should import successfully.",
              "remediationSteps": {
                "description": "In portal/services.py, function import_handoff at line 35, replace pickle-based deserialization with JSON parsing and validate that the decoded payload is a list of dict-like records before iterating over it.",
                "rational": "JSON decoding materializes only basic data types and does not execute attacker-controlled constructors during parsing. Requiring a top-level list of objects preserves the function's expected data shape while preventing pickle from reconstructing arbitrary Python objects.",
                "rationale": "JSON decoding materializes only basic data types and does not execute attacker-controlled constructors during parsing. Requiring a top-level list of objects preserves the function's expected data shape while preventing pickle from reconstructing arbitrary Python objects.",
                "suggestedFix": "    return Attachment.objects.create(ticket=ticket, original_name=upload.name, stored_name=name)\n\n\ndef import_handoff(upload):\n    rows = __import__(\"json\").loads(upload.read().decode(\"utf-8\"))\n    if not isinstance(rows, list) or not all(isinstance(row, dict) for row in rows):\n        raise ValueError(\"bundle must be a JSON array of objects\")\n    created = []\n    for row in rows:\n        subject = row.get(\"subject\", \"Delivery follow-up\")\n        description = row.get(\"description\", \"\")\n        ticket = Ticket.objects.create(\n            reference=row.get(\"reference\") or slugify(subject)[:18],\n            customer_key=row.get(\"customer_key\", \"walkup\"),",
                "verification": "Before the change, POST /imports/ with a multipart file field bundle containing a crafted pickle payload causes pickle.loads(upload.read()) to deserialize attacker-controlled bytes during import. After the change, the same pickle payload should fail JSON decoding or schema validation without deserializing Python objects, while a valid UTF-8 JSON array such as [{\"subject\":\"Test\",\"description\":\"ok\"}] should import successfully."
              }
            },
