            "severity": "Critical",
            "severityRationale": "CVSS v3.1 base score 9.8 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H, confidence: high) — AV:N (/imports/ is mounted at site root in project/urls.py and views.imports passes request.FILES['bundle'] to import_handoff over HTTP); AC:L (exploitation only requires submitting attacker-controlled pickle bytes that are deserialized immediately by pickle.loads(upload.read()) with no signature or content validation); PR:N (portal/views.py:77-80 shows no login, staff, or permission check before reaching the sink); UI:N (the attacker can upload the bundle directly and no separate victim action is needed); S:U (the unsafe deserialization executes inside the same portal application/process security authority that contains the vulnerable code); C:H (pickle.loads can execute arbitrary code during object reconstruction in the server process, permitting direct read access to application-accessible data); I:H (that same arbitrary code execution permits direct unauthorized modification/execution in the application context); A:H (attacker-executed code or even malformed pickle processing can directly crash or disrupt the service process during deserialization).",
            "severitySource": "validator",
            "specificityGap": "[deserialization] The function directly passes uploaded file content to Python's always-dangerous pickle.loads with no visible signature check, allowlist, or other pre-deserialization integrity control. Because pickle can execute attacker-controlled code during unpickling, this is a credible insecure deserialization sink.",
            "summary": "`import_handoff()` reads an uploaded file and deserializes its raw bytes with\n`pickle.loads()`. The flagged issue is that the upload comes from the public\n`/imports/` route and no repo-visible signature, authentication, or content\nvalidation is applied before deserialization. This becomes a security issue if\nrequesters can supply the `bundle` upload across that web boundary.",
