              "remediationBackwardCompat": "### Description\nIn portal/urls.py, wrap the imports route registration at line 13 with a Django permission check and import permission_required so only users with the portal.add_ticket permission can reach views.imports.\n\n### Suggested fix\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import permission_required\nfrom django.urls import path\nfrom . import views\n\napp_name = \"portal\"\n\nurlpatterns = [\n    path(\"\", views.inbox, name=\"inbox\"),\n    path(\"tickets/new/\", views.new_ticket, name=\"new_ticket\"),\n    path(\"tickets/search/\", views.search, name=\"search\"),\n    path(\"tickets/<int:ticket_id>/\", views.ticket_detail, name=\"ticket_detail\"),\n    path(\"tickets/<int:ticket_id>/reply/\", views.reply, name=\"reply\"),\n    path(\"tickets/<int:ticket_id>/attachments/\", views.upload_attachment, name=\"upload_attachment\"),\n    path(\n        \"imports/\",\n        permission_required(\"portal.add_ticket\")(views.imports),\n        name=\"imports\",\n    ),\n]\n\n### Rationale\npermission_required enforces both authentication and the named authorization check before Django dispatches to the import handler. That moves the access control decision to the network entry point, so unauthenticated users and authenticated users without ticket-creation rights cannot invoke the bulk import workflow.\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, send POST /imports/ with a multipart bundle as an anonymous client and observe that the request reaches views.imports and import_handoff processes the upload. After the change, the same anonymous request should be redirected to login or rejected, and a logged-in user without portal.add_ticket should receive a permission failure. A logged-in user granted portal.add_ticket should still be able to submit a valid bundle successfully.",
