            "cwe_name": "Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling",
            "dataFlowTrace": "`/imports/` route → `imports(request)` → `import_handoff(upload):35`\n\n- `project/urls.py:8` includes `portal.urls`\n- `portal/urls.py:13` maps `path(\"imports/\", views.imports, name=\"imports\")`\n- `portal/views.py:77-82`:\n  - checks `request.method == \"POST\"`\n  - checks `request.FILES.get(\"bundle\")`\n  - passes `request.FILES[\"bundle\"]` to `import_handoff(upload)`\n- `portal/services.py:35`:\n  - `upload` is read with `upload.read()`\n  - no size check, no `upload.chunks()`, and no repo-visible upload cap is applied first",
            "debateConsensus": "TP",
            "debateSummary": "- **TP votes:** 1\n- **FP votes:** 0\n- The available transcript is a **single-model review**, so there is no multi-model disagreement\n  record in the provided data.\n- Technical points raised in favor:\n  - `upload.read()` reads the full bundle into process memory.\n  - No in-repo size limit, streaming logic, or rejection path was found.\n  - Reachability analysis traces the input from the public `/imports/` endpoint to line 35.\n- Technical points raised on the other side:\n  - Django may spool uploaded files to disk before this function runs.\n  - A reverse proxy or deployment-specific request limit could reduce practical exposure.\n- Unresolved point:\n  - Deployment-layer limits may exist outside the repository, but none are visible in the\n    supplied code or settings.",
