              "fixDiff": "--- a/portal/services.py\n+++ b/portal/services.py\n@@ -31,8 +31,14 @@\n     return Attachment.objects.create(ticket=ticket, original_name=upload.name, stored_name=name)\n \n \n-def import_handoff(upload):\n-    rows = pickle.loads(upload.read())\n+def import_handoff(upload):\n+    max_bundle_size = 5 * 1024 * 1024\n+    if getattr(upload, \"size\", None) is not None and upload.size > max_bundle_size:\n+        raise ValueError(\"bundle too large\")\n+    payload = upload.read(max_bundle_size + 1)\n+    if len(payload) > max_bundle_size:\n+        raise ValueError(\"bundle too large\")\n+    rows = pickle.loads(payload)\n     created = []\n     for row in rows:\n         subject = row.get(\"subject\", \"Delivery follow-up\")",
              "remediationBackwardCompat": "### Description\nIn portal/services.py, import_handoff() at line 35, reject bundle files above a fixed maximum size before deserialization and bound the read call so the function does not load arbitrarily large uploads into memory.\n\n### Suggested fix\n    return Attachment.objects.create(ticket=ticket, original_name=upload.name, stored_name=name)\n\n\ndef import_handoff(upload):\n    max_bundle_size = 5 * 1024 * 1024\n    if getattr(upload, \"size\", None) is not None and upload.size > max_bundle_size:\n        raise ValueError(\"bundle too large\")\n    payload = upload.read(max_bundle_size + 1)\n    if len(payload) > max_bundle_size:\n        raise ValueError(\"bundle too large\")\n    rows = pickle.loads(payload)\n    created = []\n    for row in rows:\n        subject = row.get(\"subject\", \"Delivery follow-up\")\n\n### Rationale\nAn explicit size cap changes the importer from unbounded memory consumption to bounded memory consumption. Checking upload.size when available and also limiting read() to max_bundle_size + 1 ensures oversized inputs are rejected even if the upload object does not provide trustworthy metadata.\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, send a multipart POST to /imports/ with a very large bundle file in the bundle field; the handler reaches import_handoff(), reads the entire file with upload.read(), and process memory grows with file size. After the change, the same oversized upload should raise the \"bundle too large\" error before pickle.loads() runs, while a bundle smaller than the configured limit should still import normally.",
              "remediationSteps": {
                "description": "In portal/services.py, import_handoff() at line 35, reject bundle files above a fixed maximum size before deserialization and bound the read call so the function does not load arbitrarily large uploads into memory.",
