            "impact": "A requester could receive another customer's ticket detail page if they supply a\nvalid foreign `ticket_id`. The rendered context includes the `ticket` object and\nthe validation notes state the template displays fields such as reference,\nsubject, customer name, delivery address, robot unit, status, priority,\ndescription, notes, and attachments.",
            "prerequisites": "- The application must expose `ticket_detail` to users who are not authorized to\n  view every ticket.\n- The attacker must know or guess a valid `ticket_id` for another customer's\n  record.\n- This route is part of the default Django URL configuration, not an optional or\n  loadable extension.\n- The trust boundary is a network requester crossing into a tenant-scoped ticket\n  record identified by `Ticket.customer_key`.\n- The codebase already uses `current_customer(request)` elsewhere:\n  `inbox()` filters by `customer_key=current_customer(request)`, which indicates\n  an existing customer-scoping model.\n- The supplied threat model describes `Ticket.customer_key` as an\n  authorization-critical tenant discriminator and notes cross-tenant access as a\n  concern.\n- The related material includes a generic CWE-862 KB pattern rather than a\n  function-specific CVE; this finding matches that object-level authorization\n  pattern.",
