              "description": {
                "text": "### Description\nIn portal/services.py, update import_handoff() at lines 34-48 so Ticket.customer_key is not read from each unpickled row. Accept a trusted customer_key argument for the import operation and persist that server-side value instead of row.get(\"customer_key\").\n\n### Suggested fix\n    return Attachment.objects.create(ticket=ticket, original_name=upload.name, stored_name=name)\n\n\ndef import_handoff(upload, customer_key=\"walkup\"):\n    rows = pickle.loads(upload.read())\n    created = []\n    for row in rows:\n        subject = row.get(\"subject\", \"Delivery follow-up\")\n        description = row.get(\"description\", \"\")\n        ticket = Ticket.objects.create(\n            reference=row.get(\"reference\") or slugify(subject)[:18],\n            customer_key=customer_key,\n            customer_name=row.get(\"customer_name\", \"Caldova customer\"),\n            delivery_address=row.get(\"delivery_address\", \"Unknown route\"),\n            robot_unit=row.get(\"robot_unit\", \"RB-000\"),\n            subject=subject,\n            description=description,\n            priority=priority_for(subject, description),\n        )\n        created.append(ticket)\n    return created\r\n\n### Rationale\nUsing a caller-supplied trusted customer_key removes the tenant/queue discriminator from attacker-controlled bundle data. The imported rows can still provide descriptive fields, but the ownership field is derived outside the untrusted file so the bundle contents no longer decide which customer bucket receives the new tickets.\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, upload a bundle containing row[\"customer_key\"] = \"other-tenant\" and observe that the created Ticket row stores customer_key=\"other-tenant\". After the change, call import_handoff() with a trusted customer_key such as \"walkup\" (or the caller's current tenant) using the same bundle and verify the created Ticket row stores only the trusted value, not the value embedded in the bundle."
