            "security_severity": 5.3,
            "severity": "Medium",
            "severityRationale": "CVSS v3.1 base score 5.3 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N, confidence: high) — AV:N (the upload reaches save_attachment through the HTTP route /tickets/<int:ticket_id>/attachments/ in portal/urls.py and portal/views.py:68-72); AC:L (a normal multipart POST with attacker-controlled upload.name and file bytes directly triggers FileSystemStorage.save at portal/services.py:30, with no race or special condition shown); PR:N (upload_attachment has no authentication or permission wrapper before calling save_attachment); UI:N (the attacker can submit the upload request themselves and the file is written immediately); S:U (storage.save and Attachment.objects.create modify the same application's media storage and database, not a different security authority); C:N (the demonstrated operation persists attacker-supplied content but does not disclose existing data to the attacker); I:L (portal/services.py:30-31 writes attacker-controlled bytes/name under media/uploads and creates an Attachment row, producing unauthorized modification/addition of application data); A:N (the evidence shows file persistence, not direct service interruption or resource exhaustion).",
            "severitySource": "validator",
            "summary": "`save_attachment()` accepts an uploaded file object and writes it to `media/uploads` using the\nclient-supplied filename. The scanner flagged that the code applies no repo-visible extension,\nMIME, size, or content checks before persistence.\n\nThis becomes a security issue if an attacker can reach the upload route and the stored files are\nlater served, rendered, or processed in a context where arbitrary uploaded content matters. The\nfeature is an attachment mechanism by design; the flagged condition is the absence of restrictions\non what attachment types are accepted and how they are named on disk.",
            "triggeringInputShape": "Multipart file upload where both the file bytes and upload name are fully attacker-controlled.",
