            "cwe_name": "Improper Neutralization of Special Elements Used in a Template Engine",
            "dataFlowTrace": "`/tickets/<int:ticket_id>/reply/` → `reply()` → `render_agent_response():24-25`\n\n- `portal/urls.py:11` maps the public route to `portal/views.py:reply`\n- In `reply()`, `form = ReplyForm(request.POST)` reads the HTTP POST body\n- `form.cleaned_data[\"body\"]` is passed as `body` to `render_agent_response(...)` at line 60\n- `ReplyForm.body` is a `forms.CharField(widget=forms.Textarea)`; the provided analysis found no\n  check rejecting `{{`, `{%`, or other Django template syntax\n- `render_agent_response()` compiles `body` with `Template(body)` at line 24\n- The compiled template is rendered with:\n  - `ticket` from `get_object_or_404(Ticket, id=ticket_id)`\n  - `agent` from `request.user`\n  - `request` from the current HTTP request",
            "debateConsensus": "TP",
            "debateSummary": "- Models finding true positive: 1\n- Models finding false positive: 0\n\nThe review transcript states that attacker-controlled reply text is compiled as a Django template\nand rendered with privileged context objects, which matches CWE-1336. The same review also notes\nthat Django templates are more limited than arbitrary Python evaluation, so the expected impact is\ncontext data disclosure or content forgery rather than direct code execution.\n\nThis review was effectively single-model and unchallenged in the transcript provided, so there is\nno opposing technical argument recorded there. Separate validation data reports `debate=100%`,\n`validator=100%`, and reachability `97%`.",
