            "function": "portal/services.py:render_agent_response",
            "impact": "If the route is reachable by an untrusted actor, submitted template expressions are evaluated\nserver-side against `ticket`, `request.user`, and `request`. That can expose data reachable through\nthose objects in the rendered note content and can cause the stored note body to be derived from\nprivileged server-side state rather than the literal submitted text.\n\nThe enriched source also notes an availability side effect: malformed template syntax can raise\n`TemplateSyntaxError`, and the caller does not catch that exception in the provided path.",
            "prerequisites": "- An attacker must be able to send a POST request to `/tickets/<id>/reply/`\n- The repository evidence shows this route is included in normal URL routing, not an optional\n  extension\n- No `login_required`, permission decorator, or other code-level access check is visible on\n  `reply()` in the provided source data\n- The trust boundary crossed here is: HTTP request data → server-side template engine →\n  application objects in render context\n- The attacker is any actor who can submit reply text to this endpoint; that could be a remote\n  unauthenticated user or a lower-privileged authenticated user, depending on deployment\n- The project threat model notes `TicketNote.body` as a user-supplied persistence boundary and\n  explicitly calls out stored XSS or template injection in note content as a concern\n- The provided CVE list does not include a direct template-injection analogue; this finding is not\n  a close variant of the listed SQL injection, deserialization, or path traversal examples",
            "recommendation": {
