            "severityRationale": "CVSS v3.1 base score 6.5 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N, confidence: high) — AV:N (the /tickets/<id>/reply/ path is externally reachable over HTTP via portal/urls.py:11 and project/urls.py:8); AC:L (posting Django template syntax in ReplyForm.cleaned_data['body'] directly reaches Template(body) with no template-safety check or special precondition); PR:N (the annotated path states there is no repo-visible authentication or permission guard on reply); UI:N (the attacker’s own POST causes the server to compile and render the template, with no separate victim action); S:U (Template.render accesses ticket/request/request.user and produces note content within the same portal application authority); C:L (the render context exposes the full Ticket object, request.user, and request to attacker-authored template expressions, enabling direct server-side data disclosure); I:L (the rendered result becomes stored TicketNote content, allowing forged note/body content but not evidencing broader arbitrary execution or cross-authority modification); A:N (the only shown failure mode is a request-scoped 500 on malformed syntax, not a direct loss of service availability).",
            "severitySource": "validator",
            "summary": "`render_agent_response()` takes reply text from the `/tickets/<id>/reply/` POST flow and passes it\nto Django's `Template()` constructor. The scanner flagged this because `body` comes from\n`ReplyForm.cleaned_data[\"body\"]`, and the function renders that attacker-controlled template with a\ncontext containing `ticket`, `request.user`, and `request`.\n\nThis becomes a security issue if an external user or lower-privileged user can reach the reply\nendpoint and submit template syntax such as `{{ ... }}` or `{% ... %}`. In that case, the server\nevaluates the submitted text against privileged context objects before storing or displaying the\nresult.",
