            "function": "portal/services.py:TicketSearch.find",
            "impact": "An attacker-supplied search string can modify the `WHERE` clause logic of the search\nquery. Depending on backend behavior and surrounding authorization assumptions, this can\nreturn ticket rows outside the intended search scope, including cross-customer rows if\nthe query result is not otherwise tenant-filtered. The injected predicates may also\ncause broader scans or other single-statement SQL behavior supported by the backend.",
            "prerequisites": "- The Django route must be reachable from an external request:\n  - `project/urls.py` includes `portal.urls`\n  - `portal/urls.py:9` exposes `tickets/search/` to `views.search`\n- This code is part of the application service layer in `portal/services.py`, not an\n  optional or loadable extension.\n- Trust boundary crossed:\n  - attacker-controlled HTTP query parameter `q`\n  - application code\n  - database query execution through `connection.cursor()`\n- The attacker model is any user able to invoke the search endpoint; no prior database\n  access is required on this path.\n- The project threat model describes the application as network-facing and notes ticket\n  fields as high-risk if later used in raw SQL. It also identifies `customer_key` as a\n  tenant boundary, which is relevant if searched rows span multiple customers.\n- Related CVE note:\n  - `CVE-2022-34265` is similar at the CWE level only\n  - that CVE concerns SQL injection through crafted Django ORM expressions\n  - this finding is application-level raw SQL string construction in custom code",
