            "annotatedSource": "app_name = \"portal\" # PRECOND: Context from project/urls.py:8 mounts this module at site root (\"\"); every pattern below is directly network-reachable. PRECOND: project/settings.py:22-30 enable CsrfViewMiddleware and AuthenticationMiddleware, but portal/views.py defines no login_required or permission decorators, so authorization must be enforced inside each handler and is largely absent. HIST: File-level ranking flags this URL map as the exposure point for network-facing search, import, reply, upload, and object-ID routes (CWE-862, CWE-20).\n\nurlpatterns = [ # INV: Module-global urlpatterns is the complete dispatch table for the portal app; Django routing here only matches path strings and converts path segments, not object ownership, sink safety, or business-logic authorization. INDIRECT: Each path() entry registers a view callable that Django will invoke on a matching HTTP request.\n    path(\"\", views.inbox, name=\"inbox\"), # INDIRECT: Dispatches to views.inbox(request: HttpRequest) at portal/views.py:13-15. TAINT: The handler derives its ticket filter from current_customer(request) at portal/views.py:9-10, which reads request.COOKIES['customer_key']; inbox selection therefore depends on attacker-controlled cookie data rather than authenticated identity. PRECOND: No per-view auth check exists, so an anonymous caller can view whichever customer_key namespace they choose in the cookie.\n    path(\"tickets/new/\", views.new_ticket, name=\"new_ticket\"), # INDIRECT: Dispatches to views.new_ticket(request: HttpRequest) at portal/views.py:18-36. TAINT: POST body reaches TicketForm then Ticket.objects.create(Ticket); customer_key is client-supplied form data, so callers can mint tickets under arbitrary customer identifiers. PRECOND: No authz binds the created Ticket to request.user; CSRF middleware only blocks cross-site submission, not malicious direct use of this exposed creation endpoint.\n    path(\"tickets/search/\", views.search, name=\"search\"), # INDIRECT: Dispatches to views.search(request: HttpRequest) at portal/views.py:39-42. TAINT: GET parameter q is attacker-controlled and is passed as term into TicketSearch.find(term) in portal/services.py:11-20. DATA-FLOW: TicketSearch.find builds SQL with f-strings around term at portal/services.py:12-19 and executes it directly; this route is the network entrypoint for SQL injection (CWE-89).\n    path(\"tickets/<int:ticket_id>/\", views.ticket_detail, name=\"ticket_detail\"), # INDIRECT: Dispatches to views.ticket_detail(request: HttpRequest, ticket_id: int) at portal/views.py:45-53. TAINT: The <int:ticket_id> converter guarantees a Python int, but the object reference remains attacker-chosen. PRECOND: views.ticket_detail fetches Ticket(id=ticket_id) without filtering by customer_key or authenticated user, so this route exposes insecure direct object reference reads across tickets.\n    path(\"tickets/<int:ticket_id>/reply/\", views.reply, name=\"reply\"), # INDIRECT: Dispatches to views.reply(request: HttpRequest, ticket_id: int) at portal/views.py:56-65. TAINT: ticket_id plus POST fields author/body/status are attacker-controlled; this state-changing route is exposed without per-object authorization. DATA-FLOW: ReplyForm.cleaned_data['body'] flows into render_agent_response(ticket, body, request) at portal/services.py:23-25, which compiles attacker text as django.template.Template; this route is the entrypoint for SSTI and server-side data disclosure. PRECOND: The handler creates TicketNote(TicketNote) records and updates Ticket.status for any fetched ticket_id, so missing ownership checks permit cross-ticket tampering.\n    path(\"tickets/<int:ticket_id>/attachments/\", views.upload_attachment, name=\"upload_attachment\"), # INDIRECT: Dispatches to views.upload_attachment(request: HttpRequest, ticket_id: int) at portal/views.py:68-74. TAINT: request.FILES['file'] is attacker-controlled and is passed to save_attachment(ticket, upload) in portal/services.py:28-31. DATA-FLOW: save_attachment stores upload.name and content via FileSystemStorage(location='media/uploads') with no route-level size or type restriction; together with project/urls.py:9 static MEDIA_URL serving, uploaded content becomes directly web-reachable in DEBUG deployments. PRECOND: The handler fetches Ticket(id=ticket_id) without customer/user scoping, so attackers can attach files to arbitrary tickets.\n    path(\"imports/\", views.imports, name=\"imports\"), # INDIRECT: Dispatches to views.imports(request: HttpRequest) at portal/views.py:77-82. TAINT: Uploaded request.FILES['bundle'] is attacker-controlled. DATA-FLOW: The route passes bundle to import_handoff(upload) at portal/services.py:34-51, which executes pickle.loads(upload.read()); this is a concrete unsafe-deserialization remote code execution sink (CWE-502). PRECOND: No login, staff, or permission check guards this bulk-import surface, making the deserialization sink anonymously reachable.\n]",
