              "description": {
                "text": "### Description\nIn portal/views.py, update current_customer() at lines 9-10 to derive the tenant key from authenticated server-side user state instead of request.COOKIES, and update inbox() at lines 13-15 to deny requests when no authorized customer_key is available before running the Ticket.objects.filter query.\n\n### Suggested fix\ndef current_customer(request):\n    if getattr(request.user, \"is_authenticated\", False):\n        return getattr(request.user, \"customer_key\", None)\n    return None\n\n\ndef inbox(request):\n    customer_key = current_customer(request)\n    if not customer_key:\n        return render(request, \"portal/inbox.html\", {\"tickets\": []}, status=403)\n    tickets = Ticket.objects.filter(customer_key=customer_key).order_by(\"-updated_at\")[:50]\n    return render(request, \"portal/inbox.html\", {\"tickets\": tickets, \"is_overdue\": is_overdue})\n\n### Rationale\nUsing request.user-derived tenant data removes the client-controlled cookie as the authorization source. The added guard in inbox() prevents anonymous callers or requests without a server-authorized customer_key from reaching the ticket query, so the queryset is scoped only by trusted identity data.\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, request GET / with Cookie: customer_key=<other-tenant> returns that tenant's tickets, and GET / without the cookie falls back to cst-north-loop. After the change, the same anonymous or forged-cookie requests should return HTTP 403 with no ticket data, while an authenticated user whose request.user.customer_key matches an existing tenant should see only that tenant's tickets."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
