            ],
            "function": "portal/urls.py:<top-level>",
            "impact": "A caller can cause files to be associated with tickets other than their own, changing stored\ncase data for arbitrary ticket records. Depending on how attachments are later viewed,\nprocessed, or served, this may also expose staff workflows to attacker-supplied files or\nmisleading evidence attached to another customer's case.",
            "prerequisites": "- `project/urls.py:8` includes `portal.urls` at the site root, so this route is enabled in\n  the default web application rather than in an optional extension.\n- The caller must be able to send a POST that passes Django's CSRF checks and include a file\n  upload.\n- The caller must know or guess a valid `ticket_id`.\n- No `login_required`, `permission_required`, or similar wrapper was found on this route or\n  the related ticket views.\n- The trust boundary is an external HTTP client selecting an internal `Ticket` object and\n  causing a persistent change in attachment state.\n- The provided threat model already identifies attachment-to-ticket linkage as a broken access\n  control hotspot.\n- The related reference supplied for this finding is a generic CWE-862 KB pattern rather than\n  a specific CVE analogue for this code path.",
            "recommendation": {
              "codeAfter": "def upload_attachment(request, ticket_id):\n    ticket = get_object_or_404(\n        Ticket, id=ticket_id, customer_key=current_customer(request)\n    )\n    form = AttachmentForm(request.POST, request.FILES)\n    if request.method == \"POST\" and form.is_valid():\n        save_attachment(ticket, form.cleaned_data[\"file\"])\n        messages.success(request, \"Attachment stored.\")",
              "codeBefore": "def upload_attachment(request, ticket_id):\n    ticket = get_object_or_404(Ticket, id=ticket_id)\n    form = AttachmentForm(request.POST, request.FILES)\n    if request.method == \"POST\" and form.is_valid():\n        save_attachment(ticket, form.cleaned_data[\"file\"])\n        messages.success(request, \"Attachment stored.\")",
              "fixDiff": "--- a/portal/views.py\n+++ b/portal/views.py\n@@ -66,8 +66,10 @@\n \n \n def upload_attachment(request, ticket_id):\n-    ticket = get_object_or_404(Ticket, id=ticket_id)\n-    form = AttachmentForm(request.POST, request.FILES)\n+    ticket = get_object_or_404(\n+        Ticket, id=ticket_id, customer_key=current_customer(request)\n+    )\n+    form = AttachmentForm(request.POST, request.FILES)\n     if request.method == \"POST\" and form.is_valid():\n         save_attachment(ticket, form.cleaned_data[\"file\"])\n         messages.success(request, \"Attachment stored.\")",
              "remediationBackwardCompat": "### Description\nIn portal/views.py, update upload_attachment() around line 68 so the ticket lookup is scoped to the current customer instead of loading any Ticket by id alone.\n\n### Suggested fix\ndef upload_attachment(request, ticket_id):\n    ticket = get_object_or_404(\n        Ticket, id=ticket_id, customer_key=current_customer(request)\n    )\n    form = AttachmentForm(request.POST, request.FILES)\n    if request.method == \"POST\" and form.is_valid():\n        save_attachment(ticket, form.cleaned_data[\"file\"])\n        messages.success(request, \"Attachment stored.\")\n\n### Rationale\nScoping get_object_or_404() by both id and customer_key binds the selected Ticket to the requester context before the file is saved. This prevents a caller from choosing an arbitrary existing ticket_id and having the upload associated with another customer's record.\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, send POST /tickets/<other_customer_ticket_id>/attachments/ with a valid file while presenting a different customer_key context; the request creates an Attachment row for the other customer's ticket. After the change, the same request should return 404 (or otherwise deny access) and no attachment should be created, while a POST to a ticket owned by the current customer should still succeed.",
              "remediationSteps": {
                "description": "In portal/views.py, update upload_attachment() around line 68 so the ticket lookup is scoped to the current customer instead of loading any Ticket by id alone.",
                "rational": "Scoping get_object_or_404() by both id and customer_key binds the selected Ticket to the requester context before the file is saved. This prevents a caller from choosing an arbitrary existing ticket_id and having the upload associated with another customer's record.",
                "rationale": "Scoping get_object_or_404() by both id and customer_key binds the selected Ticket to the requester context before the file is saved. This prevents a caller from choosing an arbitrary existing ticket_id and having the upload associated with another customer's record.",
                "suggestedFix": "def upload_attachment(request, ticket_id):\n    ticket = get_object_or_404(\n        Ticket, id=ticket_id, customer_key=current_customer(request)\n    )\n    form = AttachmentForm(request.POST, request.FILES)\n    if request.method == \"POST\" and form.is_valid():\n        save_attachment(ticket, form.cleaned_data[\"file\"])\n        messages.success(request, \"Attachment stored.\")",
                "verification": "Before the change, send POST /tickets/<other_customer_ticket_id>/attachments/ with a valid file while presenting a different customer_key context; the request creates an Attachment row for the other customer's ticket. After the change, the same request should return 404 (or otherwise deny access) and no attachment should be created, while a POST to a ticket owned by the current customer should still succeed."
              }
            },
            "recommendationOrigin": "backwardCompat",
            "scanConfidence": "LIKELY",
            "security-severity": "5.5",
            "securitySeverityMethod": "cvss-base-score",
            "securitySeveritySource": "validate.severity",
            "security_severity": 5.3,
            "severity": "Medium",
            "severityRationale": "CVSS v3.1 base score 5.3 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N, confidence: high) — AV:N (project/urls.py mounts portal.urls at the site root and portal/urls.py exposes POST /tickets/<ticket_id>/attachments/ to external clients); AC:L (upload_attachment only needs an attacker-chosen ticket_id and uploaded file, and get_object_or_404(Ticket,id=ticket_id) checks existence only); PR:N (portal/views.py:68-74 has no login_required, permission check, or request.user ownership check before calling save_attachment); UI:N (the attacker can send the POST directly without any separate victim action); S:U (the write stays within the same Django application and its attachment storage/persistence boundary); C:N (the demonstrated path creates an attachment but does not directly disclose ticket or file contents to the attacker); I:L (portal/services.py:28-31 creates Attachment.objects.create(ticket=ticket, ...) for any fetched ticket_id, causing unauthorized modification of another ticket's attachment set); A:N (the evidenced operation is attachment creation, not direct service interruption).",
            "severitySource": "validator",
            "summary": "`portal/urls.py:12` registers the network-reachable route\n`/tickets/<ticket_id>/attachments/` and dispatches it to `views.upload_attachment`.\nThe flagged issue is not the route registration itself, but that the dispatched handler\nfetches a `Ticket` only by attacker-controlled `ticket_id` and then writes an attachment to\nthat ticket without an ownership check. This becomes a security issue if an external caller\ncan reach the route, satisfy CSRF handling for the POST, and guess or obtain a valid ticket ID.",
            "triggeringInputShape": "POST /tickets/<ticket_id>/attachments/ with an attacker-chosen ticket_id and uploaded file",
            "validateConfidence": "LIKELY"
          },
          "fixes": [
