            "impact": "A caller can search across ticket metadata for customers other than their own and receive up to\n25 matching rows. The reported fields include `id`, `reference`, `customer_name`, `subject`,\n`status`, and `priority`, which can expose cross-customer case information and support record\nenumeration.",
            "prerequisites": "- The route is active in the default URL configuration via `project/urls.py:8`\n  (`path(\"\", include(\"portal.urls\"))`).\n- The code shown is part of the main Django app, not an optional extension.\n- Exploitability depends on deployment intent: the issue matters if ticket search is meant to\n  be restricted to authenticated users and/or the current customer.\n- The trust boundary is network caller → ticket metadata search.\n- The attacker model is a remote HTTP caller, potentially anonymous, supplying `q`.\n- The threat model notes that `customer_key` is the tenant discriminator and identifies missing\n  authorization around it as a broken-access-control hotspot.\n- The supplied related item is a generic CWE-862 knowledge-base pattern, not a project-specific\n  CVE variant.",
