              "description": {
                "text": "### Description\nIn portal/views.py, update reply() at line 57 to scope the ticket lookup by the current customer key before processing the POST. Add a local customer_key = current_customer(request) and change get_object_or_404(Ticket, id=ticket_id) to get_object_or_404(Ticket, id=ticket_id, customer_key=customer_key).\n\n### Suggested fix\n    })\n\n\ndef reply(request, ticket_id):\n    customer_key = current_customer(request)\n    ticket = get_object_or_404(Ticket, id=ticket_id, customer_key=customer_key)\n    form = ReplyForm(request.POST)\n    if request.method == \"POST\" and form.is_valid():\n        body = render_agent_response(ticket, form.cleaned_data[\"body\"], request)\n        TicketNote.objects.create(ticket=ticket, author=form.cleaned_data[\"author\"], body=body)\n\n### Rationale\nScoping the ORM lookup to both the route-supplied ticket id and the requester’s tenant identifier ensures that only tickets belonging to the current customer are returned. The write operations that follow then apply only to records inside that authorization boundary, so an arbitrary numeric id is no longer sufficient to select another customer’s ticket.\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, set the cookie or session context for customer A and submit POST /tickets/<ticket_id_of_customer_B>/reply/ with a valid author/body/status; the handler adds a note or changes status on customer B’s ticket. After the change, the same request should return a 404 for customer A, while POSTing to a ticket id owned by customer A should still create the note and update status normally."
