            "impact": "An unauthenticated remote user could attach attacker-chosen files to an existing ticket\nselected by `ticket_id`. This can modify stored case data, consume storage, and associate\nunexpected files with another customer's ticket. Any additional effect depends on how\nstored attachments are later viewed, downloaded, or processed.",
            "prerequisites": "- Deployment conditions:\n  - The Django app must expose `project/urls.py` and `portal/urls.py` as shown.\n  - The target ticket must exist.\n  - The requester must satisfy CSRF requirements, such as by first loading the public\n    ticket detail page that renders the upload form and token.\n- Build/default status:\n  - This is a normal routed view in the main application, not an optional extension.\n- Trust boundary:\n  - Remote network user → Django view → filesystem/database persistence.\n  - The attacker model is an unauthenticated external user who can send HTTP requests and\n    choose `ticket_id` and file content.\n- Threat model alignment:\n  - The supplied threat model highlights `Ticket.customer_key` and `Attachment.ticket` as\n    authorization-sensitive boundaries.\n  - The attachment write itself is the designed purpose of the endpoint; the flagged\n    condition is the lack of gating before that write.\n- Related CVEs:\n  - The listed CVEs focus on SQL injection, traversal, deserialization, and request\n    parsing patterns. None is a close match for this missing-authentication pattern.",
