            "severityRationale": "CVSS v3.1 base score 5.3 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N, confidence: high) — AV:N (the reply sink is reached by a direct HTTP POST to /tickets/<ticket_id>/reply/ exposed in portal/urls.py:11 via project/urls.py); AC:L (ReplyForm only enforces max_length on author in portal/forms.py:13-16, and reply() writes form.cleaned_data[\"author\"] straight into TicketNote.objects.create at portal/views.py:61 with no special precondition beyond submitting the request); PR:N (the annotated source states the endpoint has no authentication or permission wrapper, so no prior privileges are required); UI:N (the attacker can submit the POST themselves and does not need a separate victim to act); S:U (the spoofed author is persisted in TicketNote in the same Django application/database security authority); C:N (this demonstrated operation stores attacker-controlled author text but does not directly disclose data to the attacker); I:L (portal/views.py:61 persists an arbitrary author value into TicketNote.author, and templates/portal/detail.html:13-15 renders that stored field as the note author, directly falsifying workflow/audit content within the portal); A:N (the evidenced behavior does not directly impair availability).",
            "severitySource": "validator",
            "summary": "`reply()` handles `POST /tickets/<ticket_id>/reply/`, validates a `ReplyForm`, and stores a\n`TicketNote` for the selected ticket. The scanner flagged line 61 because\n`form.cleaned_data[\"author\"]` comes from client-submitted form data and is persisted as the note\nauthor without being derived from `request.user` or another server-controlled identity source.\n\nThis becomes a security issue if `TicketNote.author` is treated as meaningful identity or audit\nmetadata rather than as free-form display text. The route is directly reachable through Django URL\nmapping, and the validation data did not find authentication or permission checks on this view.",
            "triggeringInputShape": "POST /tickets/<ticket_id>/reply/ with an attacker-controlled author field",
            "validateConfidence": "LIKELY"
