            "cwe_name": "Reliance on Untrusted Inputs in a Security Decision",
            "dataFlowTrace": "`POST /tickets/new/` → `new_ticket(request)` → `new_ticket():25`\n\n- Entry: `request.POST[\"customer_key\"]` reaches `TicketForm(request.POST)` at line 20.\n- Intermediate: after `form.is_valid()`, the submitted value is exposed as\n  `data[\"customer_key\"]` at line 22.\n- Sink: `Ticket.objects.create(customer_key=data[\"customer_key\"])` stores that value at\n  line 25.\n- Validation on the path: `TicketForm` limits `customer_key` to a `CharField(max_length=64)`.\n  No equality check against `current_customer(request)`, `request.user`, session state, or\n  another server-side tenant source was identified before persistence.",
            "debateConsensus": "TP",
            "debateSummary": "- Review outcome: 1 model assessed the finding as a true positive; 0 models argued false\n  positive.\n- Technical points raised for the finding:\n  - `customer_key` is persisted directly from POST data.\n  - The POST path does not overwrite `customer_key` with a server-side value.\n  - `current_customer()` is only used for GET form initialization and reads an unsigned cookie.\n- Technical point raised against the finding:\n  - The application might intentionally allow submitting tickets on behalf of any customer,\n    making `customer_key` business data rather than an authorization-bound identity field.\n- Limitation: the debate transcript contains a single-model review with a self-challenge, not a\n  multi-model split decision.",
