            "severityRationale": "CVSS v3.1 base score 5.3 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N, confidence: high) — AV:N (POST /tickets/new/ is exposed by portal/urls.py and new_ticket is a public entry point); AC:L (submitting a valid form with any attacker-chosen customer_key directly reaches Ticket.objects.create(customer_key=data['customer_key']) with no tenant-binding or authorization check); PR:N (the source states no caller-side authentication or tenant binding is enforced before ticket creation); UI:N (the attacker can send the POST themselves and no separate victim action is required); S:U (the unauthorized ticket creation occurs within the same application's ticket datastore authority); C:N (the demonstrated operation creates a record under another customer_key but does not disclose data to the attacker); I:L (Ticket.objects.create persists an attacker-supplied tenant/customer partition key, enabling forged tickets in another customer's namespace and directly compromising ticket data integrity); A:N (the evidence shows unauthorized record creation, not a direct loss of service availability).",
            "severitySource": "validator",
            "summary": "`new_ticket()` handles `POST /tickets/new/`, validates the submitted fields with `TicketForm`,\nand creates a `Ticket` row. The flagged issue is that `Ticket.customer_key` is taken directly\nfrom `form.cleaned_data[\"customer_key\"]` and persisted without a server-side binding to the\nrequester identity. This is relevant if `customer_key` is used as a tenant or customer\nboundary, which the surrounding code suggests through later filtering on that field.",
