            "annotatedSource": "def priority_for(subject, description): # TAINT: subject and description are caller-controlled: new_ticket() at portal/views.py:31 passes Django CharField-cleaned strings, but import_handoff() at portal/services.py:48 passes pickle-derived row values with no type or length validation PRECOND: callers must provide str-coercible values; this helper performs no local type checks, and the import_handoff() caller does not enforce a plain-string invariant before calling HIST: portal/workflow.py is ranked for CWE-20/CWE-862 because this helper influences workflow priority in both UI and import paths, so misclassification here can affect authorization-relevant SLA behavior\n    text = f\"{subject} {description}\".lower() # DATA-FLOW: concatenates both tainted inputs into one derived classifier string; every later decision depends solely on this lowercased text TAINT: f-string coercion invokes __format__/__str__ on untyped caller data; Python avoids memory corruption, but the import path can feed non-text objects because pickle rows are not schema-validated CONSTRAINT: runtime and temporary allocation are O(len(subject)+len(description)); new_ticket bounds subject to 180 chars via TicketForm, but description and all import-path values are effectively unbounded\n    if any(word in text for word in (\"stuck\", \"missing\", \"blocked\", \"refund\")): # TAINT: untrusted text directly controls priority escalation; any substring hit in ('stuck','missing','blocked','refund') forces the high-priority branch DATA-FLOW: uses raw substring membership rather than token or word-boundary matching, so attackers can trigger false positives with embedded terms INV: first-match-wins ordering starts here; any text containing both this branch's keywords and later urgent keywords will be classified high because this return short-circuits the function\n        return \"high\" # CONSTRAINT: returns fixed literal 'high', which is a valid member of Ticket.PRIORITY_CHOICES in portal/models.py:7\n    if any(word in text for word in (\"late\", \"cold\", \"spill\")): # TAINT: remaining tainted text can still escalate to urgent, but only if no earlier high-keyword substring matched DATA-FLOW: same substring heuristic applies here; terms like 'cold' match inside larger words and can change workflow priority without exact-token intent INV: this branch is shadowed by line 27, so mixed-keyword inputs such as 'blocked late delivery' are downgraded to high instead of reaching urgent\n        return \"urgent\" # CONSTRAINT: returns fixed literal 'urgent', which is a valid member of Ticket.PRIORITY_CHOICES in portal/models.py:7\n    return \"normal\" # CONSTRAINT: default fallback is fixed literal 'normal', also in Ticket.PRIORITY_CHOICES; this helper never returns model-supported 'low' DATA-FLOW: all inputs lacking the configured substrings collapse to normal, so caller-visible priority depends entirely on this small keyword allowlist",
