            "function": "portal/workflow.py:priority_for",
            "impact": "A requester can cause newly created tickets to be stored with `high` or `urgent` priority by\nincluding trigger words such as `refund`, `stuck`, `late`, `cold`, or `spill`. In the shown\ncode, that changes `response_due_at()` from 12 hours to 4 hours or 1 hour, which can affect\ndeadline calculations, overdue status, and any downstream prioritization tied to the stored\npriority field.",
            "prerequisites": "- The application must allow lower-trust users to create tickets or import ticket data.\n- This code is part of the normal Python service path, not an optional extension.\n- The trust boundary is between external ticket content (`request.POST` or uploaded row data)\n  and a stored internal workflow field (`Ticket.priority`).\n- The attacker model in the provided threat model is a remote unauthenticated submitter or\n  low-privilege user who can cause ticket creation/import.\n- `Ticket.priority` is later consumed by `response_due_at()`, so the classification is not\n  purely cosmetic in the shown code.\n- The provided threat model notes that `Ticket.status` and `Ticket.priority` rely on\n  application-level choices rather than database constraints.\n- The listed CVE references concern SQL injection, unsafe deserialization, path traversal, and\n  request-boundary issues; none is a close analogue to this free-text workflow escalation\n  pattern.",
