            "severityRationale": "CVSS v3.1 base score 5.3 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N, confidence: medium) — AV:N (portal/urls.py exposes the new_ticket web route, and portal/views.py:18-32 feeds POSTed subject/description into priority_for); AC:L (including one of the hard-coded substrings in attacker-controlled text is sufficient, with no race or target-specific condition); PR:N (the reachable web entry point shown in the repo applies no authentication or privilege check before creating the ticket); UI:N (the attacker submits the ticket and the server immediately computes and persists the priority without any separate victim action); S:U (the manipulated priority and derived SLA deadline remain within the same application authority); C:N (the demonstrated behavior does not disclose data to the attacker); I:L (portal/workflow.py:25-31 lets untrusted subject/description force Ticket.priority to high/urgent, and portal/views.py:23-32 persists that workflow state while response_due_at later uses it to shorten deadlines, but the change is limited to this ticket's workflow metadata); A:N (the evidence shows SLA/workflow manipulation, not direct loss of application availability).",
            "severitySource": "validator",
            "specificityGap": "[business-logic-abuse] `new_ticket()` writes `priority=priority_for(data[\"subject\"], data[\"description\"])` directly into the new ticket. `priority_for()` derives that state solely from attacker-controlled free text using raw substring checks, so the requester can select faster workflow handling by including trigger words. The mutation point has no separate invariant check against trusted order/delivery facts or any approval step before persisting the escalated state. [family: workflow-bypass-or-state-machine-break]",
            "summary": "`priority_for()` derives a ticket priority from `subject` and `description` by lowercasing the\ncombined text and checking for hard-coded substrings. The scanner flagged that this lets\nrequester-controlled text drive the persisted `Ticket.priority` state, which `response_due_at()`\nlater uses to shorten deadlines. This becomes a security issue if ticket submitters are lower\ntrust than the workflow state they can influence, and if `high` or `urgent` priorities receive\npreferential handling without separate validation.",
            "triggeringInputShape": "`request.POST` `subject` / `description` fields in `new_ticket`, e.g. any text containing substrings like `refund`, `stuck`, `late`, `cold`, or `spill`",
            "validateConfidence": "LIKELY"
          },
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "portal/workflow.py"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "endLine": 31,
                        "startLine": 25
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "def priority_for(subject, description):\n    return \"normal\""
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "### Description\nIn portal/workflow.py, replace priority_for() at lines 25-31 so ticket creation no longer derives and persists 'high' or 'urgent' priority from requester-controlled subject/description text alone.\n\n### Suggested fix\n    return [STEPS[name] for name in step.next_steps]\n\n\ndef priority_for(subject, description):\n    return \"normal\"\n\n\ndef response_due_at(ticket):\n\n### Rationale\nReturning a neutral default removes the direct path from untrusted free text to the persisted Ticket.priority workflow state. This prevents requester-supplied keywords from being treated as authoritative escalation data for downstream SLA calculations.\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, submit a new ticket with subject or description containing words such as 'refund', 'stuck', 'late', 'cold', or 'spill' and observe that the stored priority becomes 'high' or 'urgent' and response_due_at() uses the shorter deadline. After the change, submit the same inputs and observe that the stored priority remains 'normal' and response_due_at() uses the normal deadline unless some separate trusted workflow updates the priority."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
