            "function": "project/settings.py:<top-level>",
            "impact": "If the application runs with the fallback key, an attacker who knows that value can\nconstruct Django-signed data that matches the server's expected secret. Depending on the\nspecific Django feature in use, this can allow tampering with signed message data and\nother integrity-protected artifacts. Claims about authenticated session impersonation\nspecifically depend on session backend or token usage that is not fully shown in this\nrepository.",
            "prerequisites": "- `DJANGO_SECRET_KEY` must be unset or missing from the loaded `.env` file.\n- The application must be running through the normal Django WSGI/ASGI startup path, which\n  imports `project.settings` by default.\n- Public HTTP clients must be able to send or receive Django-signed artifacts that the\n  application processes.\n- This is not an optional extension; `project/settings.py` is part of default app startup.\n- The trust boundary is between remote HTTP clients and Django components that validate\n  signed data using `SECRET_KEY`.\n- The attacker model is a network client who knows the repository value and can send\n  crafted cookies, headers, or token-bearing requests.\n- The provided threat-model document is scoped to `portal/migrations/0001_initial.py` and\n  does not address secret-key management.\n- The CVEs listed in the provided threat-model section concern SQL injection,\n  deserialization, traversal, and request parsing patterns; none are a direct variant of\n  this hard-coded secret finding.",
