              "remediationBackwardCompat": "### Description\nIn project/settings.py at the top level around line 8, remove the hard-coded fallback from SECRET_KEY and fail startup when DJANGO_SECRET_KEY is not set.\n\n### Suggested fix\nBASE_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nload_dotenv(BASE_DIR / \".env\")\n\nSECRET_KEY = os.getenv(\"DJANGO_SECRET_KEY\")\nif not SECRET_KEY:\n    raise RuntimeError(\"DJANGO_SECRET_KEY must be set\")\nDEBUG = os.getenv(\"DJANGO_DEBUG\", \"1\") == \"1\"\nALLOWED_HOSTS = os.getenv(\"DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS\", \"127.0.0.1,localhost\").split(\",\")\n\nINSTALLED_APPS = [\n\n### Rationale\nRequiring DJANGO_SECRET_KEY to be supplied at startup prevents the application from using a known constant secret. Django signing operations then depend on a deployment-specific secret that is not embedded in source code, so attackers cannot derive valid signatures from the repository value alone.\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, start the app with DJANGO_SECRET_KEY unset and observe that Django boots and uses the literal caldova-local-support-key for signing. After the change, starting with DJANGO_SECRET_KEY unset should raise the RuntimeError during settings import. Then set DJANGO_SECRET_KEY to a unique value and verify the application starts normally and previously generated artifacts signed with the old fallback are no longer accepted.",
