            "severity": "Medium",
            "severityRationale": "CVSS v3.1 base score 5.3 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N, confidence: medium) — AV:N (project.urls includes portal.urls, which exposes public HTTP routes such as /tickets/search/ that can trigger request-time exceptions over the network); AC:L (settings.py sets DEBUG to true by default when DJANGO_DEBUG is unset, and portal/views.py plus portal/services.py show attacker-controlled q reaches raw SQL where a malformed term can reliably raise an exception); PR:N (the validator evidence shows /tickets/search/ is unauthenticated); UI:N (the attacker only needs to send the crafted request, with no separate user involvement); S:U (the debug page is generated by the same Django application/security authority that processes the request); C:L (with DEBUG=True, unhandled exceptions return Django's technical debug response containing stack traces and settings-derived diagnostic data to the requester); I:N (the demonstrated behavior is information exposure only, with no unauthorized modification shown); A:N (the evidence shows an error response and disclosure, not a direct loss of service availability).",
            "severitySource": "validator",
            "summary": "`project/settings.py` sets Django's `DEBUG` flag from the `DJANGO_DEBUG` environment\nvariable at module import time. The scanner flagged line 9 because an unset\n`DJANGO_DEBUG` falls back to `\"1\"`, making `DEBUG` evaluate to `True` by default.\nThis becomes a security issue when the deployment exposes HTTP routes and an\nattacker can trigger an unhandled exception, causing Django to return its\ntechnical debug response.",
            "triggeringInputShape": "Any HTTP request that triggers an application error while DJANGO_DEBUG is unset, causing DEBUG to default to true.",
            "validateConfidence": "LIKELY"
          },
